log - what the kernel and the services have said

  log                    the whole ring, oldest first
  log -n 30              the last thirty lines
  log fail               only lines containing the word
  log -f                 stay, and print what is said next
  log -f netd            the same, only the lines containing the word

Everything said since boot is recorded whether or not it was shown, so
a quiet boot can still be read back. Pipe into `page` for long output.

`log -f` is for watching a machine while something is being asked of it:
it prints what is said from that moment on, not what has already been
said, since the whole record is one command away. Ctrl+C leaves.

It waits on the record's own event, so a quiet machine costs nothing and
a boot that says a hundred things wakes it once a burst rather than once
a line. If the machine outruns it the record moves on underneath, and it
says so rather than leaving a gap nobody can see.

To read the same thing from another machine rather than this one's
screen, see `man logd`: named a serial port, the record goes out of it as
it is written.
